{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>If you lend money to My people.</b> ״A gift in secret covers anger, and a bribe in the bosom — fierce wrath״ (Proverbs 21:14). King Solomon, peace be upon him, comes to praise the quality of charity, that it repels troubles and removes punishment when it is done in secret. And 'a gift in secret' is when the giver does not know to whom he gives and the poor man also does not know from whom he takes. Now it is known that the ways of charity-givers are divided into eight parts, and they are eight levels, one above the other. <b>The first part:</b> one gives charity to a poor man but gives it with sadness. <b>The second part:</b> that he gives him less than what is proper, with a pleasant countenance. <b>The third part:</b> that he gives to the poor man into his hand before he asks. <b>The fourth part:</b> that he gives to the poor man, and the poor man knows from whom he took, but the giver does not know to whom he gave — such as the great Sages of the Talmud, who would go and wrap coins in their cloaks and sling them behind their backs, and the poor would come and take them; and they did so in order that the poor not be embarrassed. <b>The fifth part:</b> that he gives and knows to whom he gave, but the poor man does not know from whom he took — such as the great Sages of the Talmud, the supremely pious, who would go in secret and toss coins at the doorways of the poor. <b>The sixth part:</b> that he gives charity to a poor man and the giver does not know to whom he gives and the poor man does not know from whom he took — and behold, this is a mitzvah for its own sake, such as the Chamber of Secrets that was in the Temple, where the righteous would give and poor people of good family would be sustained from it in secret. And akin to this is one who gives into a charity box, which is charity given in secret: the giver does not know to whom he gives and the taker does not know from whom he takes; and this charity repels punishment. And therefore Solomon said, ״A gift in secret covers anger״ — he means that even if punishment is deserved to come upon him, the merit of giving in secret removes it from him. And behold, this is the opposite of the workings of a bribe, which is a stone of favor to the one who takes it; and thus Solomon said (Proverbs 21:15 in the printed citation; cf. Proverbs 17:8): ״A stone of favor is a bribe in the eyes of its owner; wherever he turns, he succeeds.״ And Scripture explained that one who gives a gift in secret, which deprives himself — through this very deprivation he gains, that he pushes away punishment deserved to come upon him; whereas a bribe, which he takes in his hand and brings into his bosom in secret — through this gain he loses, that he brings fierce wrath upon himself. From this you learn that this deprivation for a mitzvah brings him reward, and this gain through robbery and violence brings him loss. <b>The eighth part</b> — and it is a charity greater than them all: one who lends money to a poor man, because a loan is greater than a gift, since it sustains him in his hand and he is not embarrassed by it. Therefore we were commanded with a positive commandment in the Torah regarding the obligation of lending to a poor man, and this is what is written: 'If you lend money to My people, with the poor man who is with you.' Our Sages, of blessed memory, received: 'if' — this is an obligation; and similarly (Exodus 20:22): 'And if you make Me an altar of stone'; and similarly (Exodus 21:30): 'If a ransom is imposed upon him.' And the explanation of the verse: 'If you lend money' — you are obligated to lend money, and to whom? 'To My people' — so if your friend the non-Jew comes to borrow from you and an Israelite too [comes], the Israelite takes precedence. <b>With the poor man</b> — if a non-Jew and a poor man come to borrow from you, the poor man takes precedence; as it is said, 'the poor man who is with you.' A rich man and a poor man — the poor man takes precedence. The poor of your city and the poor of your family — the poor of your family take precedence, as it is said, 'with you.' The poor of your city and the poor of another city — the poor of your city take precedence, as it is said, 'with you' — those who are with you. 'The poor man' — 'you are poor,' that is to say, you are obligated to lend to him; and if not, know that poverty is with you — and this is 'the poor man who is with you.' <b>You shall not be to him as a creditor:</b> you shall not be to him like a lender who rules over the borrower, in the manner of what is written (Proverbs 22:7): ״ועבד לוה לאיש מלוה״ — 'and the borrower is servant to the lender.' Rather, you shall be to him in every respect as though he never borrowed money from you. <b>You shall not impose interest upon him:</b> this is monetary interest — ״נשך כסף נשך אכל״ — but rather the loan shall be an act of kindness; you shall take no benefit of honor from him, nor benefit of money — not even to greet you first, if he is not accustomed to do so. And this is what is written elsewhere (Deuteronomy 23): 'interest of anything' — even speech is forbidden." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> 'If you lend money to My people' — all the creatures of the Holy One, blessed be He, borrow one from another: day borrows from night and night borrows from day, and they do not hold each other to account as human beings do; as it is said (Psalms 19:3–4): ״יום ליום יביע אומר ולילה ללילה יחוה דעת אין אמר ואין דברים בלי נשמע קולם״. The moon borrows from the stars and the stars borrow from the moon, and when the Holy One, blessed be He, wills it, He does not let them go forth; as it is said (Job 9:7): ״האומר לחרס ולא יזרח ובעד כוכבים יחתום״. Light borrows from the sun and the sun borrows from light; as it is said (Habakkuk 3:11): ״שמש ירח עמד זבולה״. Wisdom borrows from understanding and understanding borrows from wisdom; as it is said (Proverbs 7:4): ״אמור לחכמה אחותי את ומודע לבינה תקרא״. The heavens borrow from the earth and the earth borrows from the heavens; as it is said (Deuteronomy 28:12): ״יפתח ה׳ לך את אוצרו הטוב את השמים לתת מטר ארצך בעתו״. His creatures borrow one from another and repay one another, but flesh and blood borrows from his fellow and seeks to swallow him up through interest; this is what is said (Psalms 15:5): ״כספו לא נתן בנשך ושוחד על נקי לא לקח״. Come and see: everyone who has wealth and gives charity to the poor and does not lend at interest — Scripture accounts it upon him as though he fulfilled them all; as it is said (ibid.): ״עושה אלה לא ימוט לעולם״. And who is this? This is Obadiah, who was wealthy and the steward of Ahab king of Israel; as it is said (I Kings 18:3): ״ויקרא אחאב אל עובדיהו אשר על הבית״. And he was excessively wealthy, and he expended all his property on charity, for he sustained the prophets; as it is said (ibid.): ״ואחביא מנביאי ה׳ מאה איש חמשים חמשים איש במערה ואכלכלם לחם ומים״. And when that famine came, he needed to borrow from Jehoram son of Ahab in order to save lives and so that he would have enough for the prophets, and his intention was not to give it [as interest]. This one [Obadiah] fulfilled ״כספו לא נתן בנשך״; but Jehoram, who intended in his heart to take interest — the Holy One, blessed be He, said: Let Jehu come and kill him; as it is said (II Kings 9:24): ״ויהוא מלא ידו בקשת ויך את יהורם בין זרועיו ויצא החצי מלבו״ — to fulfill what was said through Ezekiel (Ezekiel 18:13): ״בנשך נתן ותרבית לקח וחי לא יחיה״. Therefore Scripture warns: 'If you lend money.' <br>Another interpretation: <b>If you lend money to My people.</b> The Holy One, blessed be He, said: Who are 'My people'? The poor; as it is said (Isaiah 49:13): ״כי נחם ה׳ עמו וענייו ירחם״, and it is written (ibid. v. 14): ״ובה יחסו עניי עמו״. The way of flesh and blood: if he has poor relatives and he is rich, he does not acknowledge them; as it is said (Proverbs 19:7): ״כל אחי רש שנאוהו״, and it is written (ibid. v. 14 [the printed verse reference; in some editions v. 7b]): ״גם לרעהו ישנא רש״. But the Holy One, blessed be He, is not so — He is rich; as it is said (I Chronicles 29:12): ״והעשר והכבוד מלפניך״, and it is written (Haggai 2:8): ״לי הכסף ולי הזהב אמר ה׳״ — and He shelters none but the poor; as it is said (Isaiah 14:32): ״כי ה׳ יסד ציון ובה יחסו עניי עמו״. Therefore it is said: <b>If you lend money to My people, with the poor man who is with you.</b>"], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The printed citation 'Proverbs 21:15' for the verse אבן חן השוחד appears alongside the adjacent verse 21:14, but that verse's text matches Proverbs 17:8 exactly; rendered the citation as it stands in the source while noting the discrepancy. The text labels the levels as first, second, third, fourth, fifth, sixth, and then skips directly to eighth; rendered exactly as written.", 1 "The verse reference for גם לרעהו ישנא רש is printed as Proverbs 19:14, which is the same citation given for the preceding כל אחי רש (v. 7); the second verse may belong to the same verse or be a separate citation with a variant reference. Rendered as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:03:32.244150184Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}